## B. Suppressing Alpha Sticking and Multi-Technology Synergy

There are two core routes to overcoming this bottleneck: reducing the alpha-sticking probability $\omega_s$ and increasing the catalytic-cycle rate $\lambda_c$ . For the alphasticking problem, recent theoretical work has proposed a fuel-nucleus—muon dual-polarization control scheme [15]. By polarizing both the D–T fuel nuclei and the muon beam, the formation of the $(\alpha\mu)^+$ bound state may be suppressed at the quantum level. Within the assumptions of the present kinetic model, full polarization alone is estimated to bring the following performance improvements:

- the alpha-sticking probability $\omega_s$ decreases from approximately 0.45% to approximately 0.34%;
- the catalytic-cycle rate $\lambda_c$ increases by approximately 30%–50%;

• the number of catalysis cycles per muon $X_{\mu}$ increases from 148 to approximately 193.
